+# RSpec Suite Optimization Notes
+
+## Goal
+- `spec/models` + `spec/requests` together: at least 2x faster than baseline.
+- Phase 1 (this doc): `spec/models` alone, at least 30-35% faster, without touching factories or other spec types.
+- Constraints:
+  - Prefer configuration/setup-level changes (tags, shared setup, wrapping execution).
+  - Keep refactoring of individual examples minimal.
+  - What examples assert must stay the same.
+  - No app code changes affecting runtime behavior.
+  - Do NOT run whole suite or multiple dirs at once (Bash timeout). Only `rspec spec/<some-folder>` or single files for sampling.
+  - Ignore `spec/system` and streaming/search-tagged specs (excluded by default).
+
+## Baseline Metrics (spec/models)
+
+Measured 2026-10-06, seed 1234, `Finished in X seconds (files took ~1.9s to load)` is run time excluding load.
+Total examples in `spec/models`: 1795 (`rspec spec/models --dry-run`).
+
+Sampled heavy files (seed 1234):
+| File | Examples | Time |
+|------|----------|------|
+| spec/models/account_spec.rb | 122 | 4.04s |
+| spec/models/status_spec.rb | 62 | 7.44s |
+| spec/models/account_statuses_cleanup_policy_spec.rb | 36 | 8.79s |
+| spec/models/public_feed_spec.rb | 43 | 5.13s |
+| spec/models/tag_spec.rb | 70 | 1.07s |
+| spec/models/notification_spec.rb | 25 | 4.60s |
+| spec/models/user_spec.rb | 72 | 3.32s |
+| spec/models/media_attachment_spec.rb | 27 | 6.61s |
+| spec/models/report_spec.rb | 17 | 2.11s |
+| spec/models/collection_spec.rb | 31 | 1.71s |
+| spec/models/featured_tag_spec.rb | 15 | 1.22s |
+
+Sampled light files:
+| File | Examples | Time |
+|------|----------|------|
+| account_alias_spec.rb | 3 | 0.73s |
+| canonical_email_block_spec.rb | 15 | 0.80s |
+| domain_block_spec.rb | 21 | 0.74s |
+| invite_spec.rb | 23 | 1.01s |
+| poll_spec.rb | 13 | 1.01s |
+| mute_spec.rb | 14 | 0.97s |
+| block_spec.rb | 7 | 1.03s |
+| favourite_spec.rb | 3 | 1.18s |
+
+Estimated total `spec/models` runtime: ~90-120s (avg ~0.05-0.08s/example * 1795).
+Do not run full `spec/models` in one Bash call if it risks timeout; use sampling.
+
+## Profiling Insights
+
+### 1. Sidekiq `inline` + `TriggerWebhookWorker` dominates fabrication cost (MAIN WIN)
+- `spec/rails_helper.rb` sets `Sidekiq.testing!(:inline)` globally before each test.
+- Every `Fabricate(:account)` (via its `user`) enqueues 1x `TriggerWebhookWorker` (`account.created`).
+- Every `Fabricate(:status)` enqueues 3x in default case (status + status.account's user + bookmark.account's user via `status` fabricator's `after_create { Fabricate(:bookmark) }`), or 2x when sharing `account:` (status + bookmark.account).
+- `TriggerWebhookWorker#perform` does `find` + `WebhookService#call` (serializes via `REST::Admin::WebhookEventSerializer` + `Webhook.enabled` query). Direct cost ~0.004s, but with overhead ~0.010-0.015s per job in context.
+
+Benchmarks (outside RSpec, warmed, transaction rollback):
+- `Fabricate(:account)` x5: inline 0.0256s avg vs fake 0.0104s avg (59% reduction). Stubbing `WebhookService` in inline gives 0.011s avg (same as fake).
+- `Fabricate(:status)` (new account) x5: inline 0.0607s vs fake 0.0289s (52% reduction).
+- `Fabricate(:status, account: shared)` x10: inline 0.0363s vs fake 0.0149s (59% reduction, saves ~0.021s/status).
+- `Fabricate(:user)` x5: inline 0.0191s vs fake 0.0139s (27% reduction).
+- `Fabricate(:media_attachment)` (default `attachment.jpg`): 0.094s vs remote-only (file nil): 0.011s. File processing adds ~0.083s each (see #3).
+
+Implication: stubbing `TriggerWebhookWorker.perform_async` for `type: :model` should give ~30-50% speedup on status/account-heavy specs, ~25-30% overall (media specs benefit little).
+
+Safety:
+- No `spec/models` example asserts `TriggerWebhookWorker` via `enqueue_sidekiq_job`; only via `allow(...).to receive(:perform_async)` + `have_received` (in `user_spec.rb`, `user/confirmation_spec.rb`). Global stub + spec-level re-stub is compatible (spec stub wins, counts after it).
+- Mailer specs (`email_subscription_spec.rb`, `user_spec.rb#mark_email_as_confirmed!`, `admin/account_action_spec.rb` `capture_emails`) REQUIRE `inline` (verified: switching to `fake` breaks them, 0 emails vs 1). Therefore do NOT switch model specs to `fake` globally; keep `inline` and stub only selected workers.
+- Do NOT stub workers asserted via `enqueue_sidekiq_job` / `jobs.size` in models:
+  - `CacheBusterWorker` (media_attachment_spec)
+  - `LocalNotificationWorker`, `Admin::SuspensionWorker` (admin/account_action_spec)
+  - `Fasp::*` (fasp_concern specs, only enqueued when `feature: :fasp`)
+  - `AddToPublicStatusesIndexWorker` etc are stubbed in their own specs; Chewy disabled elsewhere so no benefit.
+
+Other workers safe to stub (not asserted via enqueue, only via `have_received` with stub or not asserted):
+- `BootstrapTimelineWorker` (user approve/confirm path, does staff query)
+- `MergeWorker` (+ `push_bulk`) (follow_request authorize, does feed merge)
+- `PublishAnnouncementReactionWorker` (announcement_reaction create, does DB+render+Redis)
+- `PushConversationWorker`, `PostProcessMediaWorker` (rarely enqueued in models, harmless to stub but low benefit; include for completeness if needed)
+
+Tag for opt-out: `sidekiq_inline: true` (or `sidekiq: :inline`) on examples that truly need real inline execution of stubbed workers. Default is stubbed for `type: :model`.
+
+### 2. Redis cleanup `redis.del(redis.keys)` vs `flushdb`
+- `rails_helper.rb` `config.after { Rails.cache.clear; redis.del(redis.keys) }`.
+- For models, typically 0-1 keys (`activity:statuses:local:*`), cost negligible (~0.00003s). `flushdb` is cleaner/faster with many keys and avoids `KEYS` blocking, but not a big win for models. Consider changing globally in later phase (affects all types) or leave for requests phase. Do NOT change globally in Phase 1 to avoid touching other types.
+
+### 3. Paperclip / MediaAttachment processing
+- Default `Fabricate(:media_attachment)` processes `attachment.jpg` (60K) via ImageMagick/VIPS + blurhash etc: ~0.094s vs 0.011s remote-only.
+- Only 2 non-media model specs create media_attachments:
+  - `status_spec.rb#ordered_media_attachments` (4 per test, 2 tests = 8 total)
+  - `account_statuses_cleanup_policy_spec.rb` (1 per `statuses_to_delete` test, ~15 tests)
+- Potential saving ~1-2s total if stubbed, but stubbing Paperclip processors is risky/complex. Leave for later; document as future opportunity. Do NOT stub in `media_attachment_spec.rb` itself (it tests processing).
+
+### 4. Faker
+- `Faker::Internet.user_name`: 100x = 0.36s (0.0036s each). Per `Fabricate(:account)` adds ~0.0036s. For ~1000 accounts, ~3.6s.
+- `Faker::Internet.email`: 100x = 0.014s (negligible).
+- Factories phase will address (use sequences/fast stubs). Do NOT touch in Phase 1 per instructions, but note as config-level possibility (monkey-patch Faker) if needed.
+
+### 5. Factory-level (DO NOT TOUCH IN PHASE 1, for later phase)
+- `status` fabricator `after_create { Fabricate(:bookmark, status: status) }` creates extra bookmark + extra account (+user+login_activities+webhooks) per status. Likely injected slowdown; real Mastodon does not do this. Removing/conditionalizing will be huge (saves ~0.01s + webhooks per status).
+- `user` fabricator `after_create { 2.times { Fabricate(:login_activity) } }` adds 2 DB inserts per user. Similarly suspicious.
+- `bookmark`/`favourite` fabricators use `Fabricate.build(:account/status)` but when `status:` is passed, `account` is still built (extra account). Could use shared/transient accounts.
+- `account` fabricator already uses static test keys (avoids RSA 2048 generation, good).
+
+### 6. Other checked (negligible)
+- `ActivityTracker.increment`: 100x = 0.0049s (0.00005s each) - negligible.
+- `Account#increment_count!` (upsert): 20x = 0.007s - negligible.
+- `Rails.cache.clear` (memory_store): cheap.
+- `Rails.application.reload_routes_unless_loaded`: no-op after load.
+- `allow(Resolv::DNS)...`: cheap.
+- `Chewy.strategy(:bypass)`: already optimal (disabled unless search specs).
+- `Devise.stretches = 1` in test: already optimal.
+- `Fasp` disabled by default: no jobs.
+- `Account#generate_keys` (RSA 2048) avoided via static keys in fabricator: good.
+
+## Plan for Phase 1 (spec/models only)
+
+1. In `spec/rails_helper.rb`, add `config.before(:each, type: :model)` to stub expensive Sidekiq workers unless tagged `sidekiq_inline: true`:
+   - `TriggerWebhookWorker.perform_async` (main)
+   - `BootstrapTimelineWorker.perform_async`
+   - `MergeWorker.perform_async` + `push_bulk`
+   - `PublishAnnouncementReactionWorker.perform_async`
+   - (Optionally `PushConversationWorker`, `PostProcessMediaWorker` if measured beneficial)
+2. Verify on sampled heavy files (same seed 1234) that all pass and show 30%+ improvement on average (excluding `media_attachment_spec.rb` which is processing-bound).
+3. Check edge cases:
+   - `user_spec.rb` approve/confirmation webhook expectations still pass (re-stub).
+   - `email_subscription_spec.rb`, `user_spec.rb#mark_email_as_confirmed!`, `admin/account_action_spec.rb` mailer tests still pass (inline preserved).
+   - `media_attachment_spec.rb` CacheBuster enqueue tests still pass (not stubbed).
+   - `follow_request_spec.rb`, `relay_spec.rb`, `statuses_search_spec.rb` stub+expect tests still pass.
+   - `fasp_concern` specs (feature: :fasp, fake) still pass.
+4. Do NOT change factories, do NOT change app code runtime behavior, do NOT affect other spec types (scope to `type: :model`).
+5. Future phases:
+   - Factories: remove bookmark-per-status, login_activities-per-user, optimize Faker, reuse accounts.
+   - Requests: likely similar webhook stubbing + Redis flushdb + media stubbing + Faker; measure separately.
+   - Consider `redis.flushdb` globally, `Faker` fast stubs, Paperclip processor stubs for non-media specs.
+
+
+## Phase 1 Implementation (2026-10-06, spec/models only)
+
+### Changes
+1. `spec/rails_helper.rb`: added `config.before(:each, type: :model)`:
+   - `allow(TriggerWebhookWorker).to receive(:perform_async)` unless `sidekiq_inline: true`.
+     - Minimal single stub (tried 7 workers, but extra 6 added 0.00048s/test overhead each for negligible benefit; only `TriggerWebhookWorker` is triggered broadly, others 0 jobs in typical fabrications).
+     - Keeps `Sidekiq.testing!(:inline)` (required for `capture_emails`).
+   - Opt-in `media_processing: :stub` tag to skip `Paperclip::Attachment#post_process` for `MediaAttachment` only (via `allow_any_instance_of...and_wrap_original` with `is_a?(MediaAttachment)` check). Other attachments (Account avatar) still real.
+2. `spec/models/media_attachment_spec.rb`: added `media_processing: :stub` to 2 contexts (4 examples):
+   - `needs_redownload?` `when file is present` (2 tests, each did `MediaAttachment.new(file: avatar.gif)` costing 0.46s for processing, but only asserts `file.blank?` logic).
+   - `#to_param` (2 tests, each did `Fabricate.build(:media_attachment)` costing 0.06s, but only asserts `to_param`).
+   - No assertion changes, only tags.
+3. No factory edits, no app code changes, no other spec types touched (rails_helper change scoped to `type: :model`; verified `spec/requests/...` still passes).
+
+### Optimized Metrics (seed 1234, same files)
+Combined runs (more accurate than sum of singles due to shared warmup; `Finished` excludes load):
+
+| Sample | Baseline | Optimized | Improvement |
+|--------|----------|-----------|-------------|
+| 3 heavy: account+status+user (256 ex) | 13.01s | 8.77s | 32.6% |
+| 3 heavy: cleanup+public_feed+notification (104 ex) | 16.77s | 10.76s | 35.8% |
+| 7 heavy (no media): account,status,cleanup,public_feed,tag,notification,user (430 ex) | 29.42s | 20.33s (full7) / 19.4s (minimal Trigger-only) | 30.9% / **34.1%** |
+| 8 with media (457 ex): above + media_attachment | 36.31s | 27.19s (full7) / 25.52s (minimal) / **24.85s** (minimal+media tags) | 25.1% / 29.7% / **31.6%** |
+| 8 light (99 ex): alias,canonical,domain_block,invite,poll,mute,block,favourite | 2.43s | 1.89s (minimal) | **22.2%** |
+| 8 medium (92 ex): collection,report,featured_tag,follow,list,poll_vote,instance,conversation | 4.65s | 3.32s | **28.6%** |
+| tag_feed alone (33 ex) | 7.99s | 5.10s | **36.2%** |
+| media_attachment alone (27 ex) | 6.61s | 5.50s (with 4 tags) | **16.8%** (was 1.6% without tags) |
+
+Overall estimate for full `spec/models` (1795 ex, media 7% of time):
+- Non-media (~93% time) at ~30-34% (heavy 34%, medium 28%, light 22% weighted ~30%+).
+- Media (7% time) at 16.8%.
+- Weighted overall **~30-32%**, meets 30-35% target. Single-file sums underestimate due to per-file warmup; combined runs are authoritative.
+
+### Lessons / Dead Ends (for future phases)
+- **Faker**: `Faker::Internet.user_name` first call 0.35s (cold, loads locales), next 100 calls 0.0078s (0.078ms each). Steady-state negligible. Stubbing via `allow(...).to receive(:user_name)` adds 0.00048s/test overhead for ~0.00008s saving → net negative (measured stubbed slower than real in RSpec). Do NOT stub Faker via `allow`; factories phase should replace with sequences in files (no per-test overhead).
+- **Paperclip broad stub**: `allow_any_instance_of(Paperclip::Attachment).to receive(:post_process)` for all model specs adds overhead (~0.002s/test) outweighing benefit for files without media (account_spec slowed 3.2→3.44s). Restricted to 2 files still marginal due to variance. Final: opt-in tag only for 4 wasteful tests (1s saved in media file, no overhead elsewhere).
+- **Extra Sidekiq stubs**: `Bootstrap`, `Merge`, `PublishAnnouncement`, `PushConversation`, `PostProcess` are 0 jobs in typical fabrications (verified via fake counting: only Trigger 14 + Publish 1 for 8 fabrications). Stubbing them adds 6*0.00048*1795≈5s overhead for <0.5s benefit. Kept only `TriggerWebhookWorker` (broadly triggered, 1-3 per fabrication). Minimal 19.4s vs full7 20.33s for 430 ex (4.5% better).
+- **Logging**: `Rails.logger` DEBUG (62M test.log) – setting to WARN showed no win (DEBUG 0.52s vs WARN 0.65s for 20 statuses, noise). Do not pursue.
+- **Eager load**: `CI=true` (eager) gives 2.54s vs 3.19s runtime for account_spec but +1.77s load. CI already eager, so no additional win.
+- **Redis `del(keys)` vs `flushdb`**: negligible for models (0-1 keys, 0.00003s). Leave for requests phase (more keys).
+- **LoginActivity/Bookmark**: 2 inserts per user, 1+account per status – factory phase (removing from fabricator files, not via `allow_any_instance_of` which would add overhead).
+
+### Safety Verification (all pass, seed 1234)
+- Webhook expectations: `user_spec.rb`, `user/confirmation_spec.rb` (re-stub, `have_received` with `.once`/`not`).
+- Mailers (`capture_emails`, require inline): `email_subscription_spec` (4 ex), `user_spec#mark_email_as_confirmed`, `admin/account_action_spec` (15 ex, incl. `capture_emails` + `fake` enqueue checks for `Suspension`/`LocalNotification`).
+- Enqueue checks (not stubbed): `media_attachment` CacheBuster, `admin/account_action` Suspension/LocalNotification, `fasp_concern` (feature: :fasp, fake), `statuses_search` (stub+expect, Chewy mocked).
+- Other: `follow_request`, `relay` (stub+expect), `remotable` (Rails.logger.debug), `account_conversation`, `announcement`, `custom_filter`, `invite`, `marker`, `poll`, `report`, `status_pin`, `tag_feed`, `home_feed`, plus 1 request spec (`api/v1/accounts`, 39 ex) to confirm no cross-type impact.
+
+### Future (requests + factories)
+- Requests: likely same Trigger stub helps (many account/status fabrications via API auth). Measure separately; also consider `flushdb`, media stub for request specs that upload files (but many assert processing – need tags).
+- Factories: remove `status`→`bookmark`, `user`→`2x login_activity`, replace `Faker::Internet.user_name` with fast sequences in files (not via `allow`), reuse accounts for bookmark/favourite.
+- Keep tags `sidekiq_inline: true` and `media_processing: :stub/:real` for documentation; factories phase should preserve them.
+
